1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,866
Every flying machine comes with a manual.

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:07,354
This one is for a rocket that hasn't flown.

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:17,252
Project 33 lives on a workbench, and this manual authorizes no ignition, no propulsion, and no flight.

4
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:26,201
Flight characteristics: not measured.

5
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,721
Performance data: not measured.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,501
Here, that isn't a gap. It's the status.

7
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:43,246
Project 33 has four subsystems: the airframe, the rocket's flight computer, the launcher, and a laptop dashboard.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,792
The dashboard talks to the launcher over Wi-Fi.

9
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:51,332
The launcher talks to the rocket over a serial wire.

10
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:58,519
Inside the rocket, an ESP32 reads a motion sensor and moves four small canards.

11
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:01,080
Its one job would be to stop the roll.

12
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,203
No steering. No guidance. Just steady.

13
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:11,442
Normal procedures start without hardware.

14
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:14,809
The safest first experiment is reading the record.

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,839
Python 3.11 or newer. Run status. Run check.

16
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:22,673
Build the review, verify it, and open it in a browser.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,635
Verification proves the packet is intact. Not that the rocket works.

18
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:36,935
Before power: ignition servo mechanically disconnected. No energetic material on the bench.

19
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:41,014
Servos get their own supply, sized for stall current.

20
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:45,258
Wiring, verified. Firmware, built from a recorded commit.

21
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:50,613
Dashboard open, and labeled RAW SESSION, because it isn't evidence yet.

22
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:54,037
Arm switch on: the launcher reaches READY.

23
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:58,376
Arm switch off: back to SAFE, and the abort is written down.

24
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,210
Ask the dashboard to launch…

25
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:04,667
It refuses.

26
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:06,004
Good.

27
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,561
Each canard has its own center angle in firmware, and a limit of twelve degrees either way.

28
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:22,693
Hold the rocket still and calibrate. The firmware won't confirm it; the dashboard only records that you asked.

29
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:28,258
Then compare gains: one setting per session, the same fixture motion every time.

30
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:34,486
Close the dashboard cleanly. Declare what you did. Build a passport.

31
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,618
Then a named human decides whether it counts.

32
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,496
Software can organize evidence. It cannot accept it.

33
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:55,324
If rocket telemetry stops but the launcher keeps talking, open the status board and press DUMP LOG.

34
00:02:55,750 --> 00:03:00,805
A reply clears the serial wire and the rocket, and points to the motion sensor.

35
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:03,730
Silence points to the wire or the rocket.

36
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:09,245
Every failure leaves a map.

37
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,171
Now the limits.

38
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:20,360
In its own model, the folding-fin linkage, as specified, doesn't close.

39
00:03:20,750 --> 00:03:25,367
The spring margin is nineteen percent, against a twenty-percent requirement.

40
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:27,780
Two stability models disagree.

41
00:03:28,250 --> 00:03:31,597
No physical measurement has been accepted. Not one.

42
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:36,592
The record keeps all of it. The contradiction is part of the result.

43
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:44,417
No claim has gone past the synthetic test. From here on, every gate needs a person.

44
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:52,408
Build a model. Make a prediction. Measure the difference. Keep the record honest.

45
00:03:54,417 --> 00:03:55,539
Run the review.
